# Gates deliberately shipped without a fixture, one name per line, spelled
# exactly as the script echoes it. `gate coverage` in bin/check-fast reads this
# file and refuses a gate that is neither covered here nor covered by a fixture,
# so every name below is a decision somebody made on purpose and wrote down -
# not a gap nobody noticed. An entry that stops matching a real gate, or that a
# fixture has since covered, is itself refused: this list has to shrink when a
# fixture lands. tests/gates/README.md carries the long form of each argument.
#
# All four need a compiled workspace, and a cold CXX-Qt build on this host is
# over twenty minutes. All four are also stock invocations of tools whose
# failure behaviour is not in question, which is the difference between them and
# the hand-written shell every other gate is made of.

# Needs a full build per fixture, and clippy and test invalidate each other's
# cxx-qt fingerprints, so one fixture costs two rebuilds.
clippy

# The same rebuild, plus a fixture would have to break a test to prove the
# runner reports it - which cargo test demonstrates on every red run anyway.
workspace tests

# Runs the whole suite four more times; the property it guards is proven by the
# suite it re-runs, not by the loop around it.
timezone matrix

# An instrumented rebuild, and the threshold is a number cargo llvm-cov enforces
# rather than a check written here.
core coverage
